<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian front (World War I)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Fronte italiano, Südwestfront</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian front was one of the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, a series of engagements along the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> from 1915 to 1918. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> entered the war on the Entente side to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trento</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, Istria and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, promised to it in the 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a>. The front bogged down into trench warfare, much of it at high altitudes, and several thousand civilians died in refugee camps. Fighting ended with Italian victory and the armistice of Villa Giusti on 4 November 1918, and Italy annexed Trento, Trieste, South Tyrol and Istria.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had been allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> in the Triple Alliance, and claimed the alliance was void because the treaty was defensive and the Germans had attacked first. In the Bosnian Crisis of 1908 Italy expected compensation in the Italia Irredenta under the alliance treaties, and none came. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> of 1911 weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and alienated Italy from its partners.</p><p>The front ran about 600 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stelvio-pass">Stelvio Pass</a> along the Tyrolean, Carinthian and Littoral borders to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Isonzo</a>, of which 450 kilometres lay in high alpine terrain. Ridge heights reached 2,700 to 3,200 metres, and the rocky ground made trenches hard to dig. In the karst of the Isonzo valley, exploding grenades threw off fragments of rock as additional shrapnel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1915 the high peaks of the Dolomites saw fierce mountain warfare, and both sides built fighting tunnels for cover and supply, such as the Italian Strada delle 52 Gallerie. They also tunnelled under no man's land to place charges beneath enemy positions: between 1 January 1916 and 13 March 1918 they fired 34 mines, with focal points at Pasubio, Lagazuoi, Col di Lana and Marmolada. Beginning on 13 December 1916, later called White Friday, avalanches killed 10,000 soldiers on both sides, some set off deliberately by artillery fire.</p><p>In 1917 Italian forces under Luigi Capello took the Banjšice Plateau, but their supply lines failed to keep pace. German troops brought infiltration tactics to the Austro-Hungarian side, and on 24 October 1917 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caporetto">Battle of Caporetto</a> began with a gas-shell barrage. By the end of the first day the Italians had retreated 19 kilometres to the Tagliamento River. Armando Diaz, the new chief of staff, halted the retreat around Monte Grappa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Italians were pushed back to the Piave River, having suffered 600,000 casualties by then, and called up the class of 1899, the Ragazzi del '99. In the Second Battle of the Piave in June 1918 Austrian deserters betrayed the plan of the offensive, and Italian reinforcements and air attacks on supply lines halted it. At Vittorio Veneto, in October and November 1918, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> was collapsing, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> proclaimed independence. Italian military deaths numbered over 531,000, including prisoners and the missing.</p><p>The treaties of 1919 and 1920 gave Italy Trento, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a> and Istria, while northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, except the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a>, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. The annexation of Trento and Trieste is seen as the culmination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, and the intervention is sometimes called the Fourth Italian War of Independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italian front (World War I)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
